.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1200 PM PST Thu Nov 5 2026
(This evening through Friday night)

Temperatures will be on a gradual warming trend through the
remainder of the week as high pressure builds in from the Eastern
Pacific. As such, afternoon temperatures will be up to 2-8 deg F
above seasonal averages today in the major population centers. High
clouds will be streaming in from the northwest throughout the
afternoon and evening hours, having little effect on temperatures.

Not seeing much in the way of fog development tonight, as weak to
moderate offshore flow persists across the higher terrain of the Bay
Area and Central Coast. However, cannot rule out a few pockets of
fog in the North Bay valleys early Friday morning and lingering
until the sun rises before quickly mixing out. Once again, high
clouds are forecast to stream in from the northwest on Friday
morning and then clearing out by mid-to-late afternoon. As such,
daytime high temperatures on Friday look to be a few degrees warmer
than today.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1200 PM PST Thu Nov 5 2026
(Saturday through next Wednesday)

Weak ridging or zonal flow will persist across the region through
early next week, leading to slightly above normal temperatures. This
will be both overnight minimums and afternoon maximums. A shallow
marine layer, generally less than 1,000 feet, is forecast to return
Saturday night into Sunday morning ahead of an approaching frontal
system set to move into northern California. Confidence in wet
weather conditions have stayed the same if not lessened slightly
heading into the middle of next week and beyond. Again, the ECMWF
ensemble mean shows greater than 2" of rain at SFO between Wednesday
the 11th and Wednesday the 18th. While that is about normal for the
interior month, it looks to be spread out in time. However, given
the lack of confidence looking out this far in time, be sure to
check back for the latest forecast information.

