.AVIATION...
(18Z TAFS)
Issued at 942 AM PST Thu Nov 5 2026

Patchy ground fog and stratus has eroded this morning,
leaving us with clear skies and VFR conditions while writing this
discussion. Looking at satellite, high clouds are slowly creeping
into northwestern CA and should slowly sag into the Bay Area late
this morning and into the afternoon hours. Models depict high clouds
clearing by the evening and early overnight hours, leaving us with
mainly clear skies overnight. The forecast challenge for the period
will be if we get any fog or low cloud development tonight. Given
the trends at KAPC and KSTS, opted to continue to hint at some
patchy ground fog and low cloud from roughly 11Z-15Z, though the
onset could be a bit earlier. Expect VFR conditions for all
terminals shortly after sunrise / by mid morning. High clouds look
to return to the forecast. Moderate to high confidence in the
forecast.

Vicinity of SFO...Light offshore winds should prevail through the
morning and into the early afternoon hours. The seabreeze is
expected to return this afternoon, likely between 22Z and 0Z, where
winds will generally be around 10 to 15kt. Winds ease into the
evening hours, becoming light and potentially variable after 6Z-8Z
time frame. Weak offshore flow develops mid morning, with onshore
flow returning tomorrow afternoon. VFR conditions prevail, and we`ll
see a mix of clear skies and high clouds through the period.
Moderate to high confidence in the forecast.

SFO Bridge Approach...Similar to SFO.

Monterey Bay Terminals... VFR conditions are expected to continue
through the TAF period, with low confidence of impactful stratus
developing this evening into Friday morning. Light winds out of the
northwest will shift to light south-southeast winds in the afternoon
and evening hours before transitioning back to northwest Friday
morning.

